    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d332">

                <pb facs="0719" n="707" sameAs="frus:frus1946v09/pg707"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Marshall Mission Files, Lot 54–D270:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Colonel J. Hart Caughey to Brigadier General Thomas S.
                        Timberman</hi><anchor n="30" xml:id="d332-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Nanking</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1946-06-08">8 June 1946</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>852. In letter to General Marshall from General Chou dated 6 June<anchor n="31"
                        xml:id="d332-f2"/> he says that General Fang Fang, Communist representative
                    in Canton, reports that many obstacles by National Government, concerning food
                    and money, is delaying Communist assemblage at Tapeng Peninsula 1 week. General
                    Chou requests “U. S. vessels at Tapeng to stand by until embarkation is
                    effected”. Request that you take appropriate action.<anchor n="32"
                        xml:id="d332-f3"/></p>
                <closer><signed>J. <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hart Caughey</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d332-f1" n="30" type="source" xml:id="d332-f1-content">
                <p>Acting Director of Operations of Executive Headquarters at Peiping, during
                    Brigadier General Byroade’s absence at Changchun.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d332-f2" n="31" xml:id="d332-f2-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d332-f3" n="32" xml:id="d332-f3-content">
                <p>General Chou was informed of this action by General Marshall in his memorandum
                    No. OSE 137, June 8, not printed.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
